Okay, I need to explain how the immune system works. Let me start by recalling what I know. The immune system is our body's defense against pathogens like bacteria, viruses, fungi, and parasites. It has two main branches: innate and adaptive. 

First, the innate immune response. That's the first line of defense, non-specific. It includes physical barriers like skin and mucus. Then there are chemical barriers, like stomach acid. When pathogens get in, the innate system responds quickly. Neurons in the brain send signals to cells that release chemicals. These include cytokines, which are signaling molecules, and chemokines, which attract immune cells. There's also the complement system, a group of proteins that help destroy pathogens by forming pores or attracting antibodies. Oh, and macrophages and neutrophils are part of this—they're phagocytes that engulf and kill pathogens.

Then the adaptive immune response, which is more specific. It takes longer but is more targeted. It involves lymphocytes—B cells and T cells. B cells produce antibodies, which are proteins that bind to specific antigens (like parts of pathogens). T cells, on the other hand, help coordinate the response. There's helper T cells (Th cells) that activate other cells, cytotoxic T cells (Tc cells) that kill infected cells, and regulatory T cells (Tregs) that prevent overreaction.

I should mention antigen presentation. Antigens are foreign substances, and they need to be presented to T cells by antigen-presenting cells (APCs) like dendritic cells, macrophages, and B cells. This process involves MHC molecules—major histocompatibility complex, which helps T cells recognize the antigen.

Also, memory cells. After an infection, some B and T cells become memory cells, which can quickly respond if the same pathogen re-enters. That's why vaccines work—they stimulate the immune system to make memory cells without causing disease.

